# Paint & Color Chemistry Curriculum Cache
#
# Linseed autoxidation cross-linking, refractive scattering, lapis ultramarine, and the collapsible tube revolution.

#>spw_cache_mat_paint
#:cache #!paint_and_color_chemistry
#:layer #!pragmatics

^"intent"{
  ~#goal: "Model paint formulation physics, polyunsaturated fatty acid drying autoxidation, pigment refractive scattering, the history of mineral and synthetic pigments, and the Impressionist tube revolution."
  ~#protocol: "cache.layer/1"
}

^"formulation_chemistry_and_film_formation"{
  "four_component_system_and_refractive_opacity": .{
    paint_system_architecture: "Pigment (insoluble light-scattering/absorbing particles) + Binder/Vehicle (polymeric film former) + Solvent (volatile diluent governing viscosity) + Additives (driers, plasticizers, biocides, wetting agents)",
    fresnel_refractive_scattering_and_hiding_power: "Opacity and hiding power governed by refractive index mismatch between pigment and binder: Delta n = |n_pigment - n_binder|; Titanium Dioxide (TiO2 rutile n = 2.73) in linseed oil (n = 1.48) produces immense light scattering; calcium carbonate chalk (n = 1.58) in oil matches binder index, rendering it virtually transparent",
  }[reg=facet],
  "curing_mechanisms_and_drying_oil_autoxidation": .{
    linseed_oil_oxidative_polymerization: "Drying oils (linseed, walnut, poppyseed) rich in polyunsaturated fatty acids (linolenic, linoleic acids); curing is not evaporation, but chemical autoxidation: atmospheric O2 attacks allylic methylenes, forming hydroperoxides that cleave into free radicals driving cross-linking into a resilient 3D polymeric net (linoxin); paint film gains up to 15% weight from absorbed oxygen",
    egg_tempera_and_true_buon_fresco: "Egg tempera: egg yolk lipid-protein emulsion curing via water loss and slow protein cross-linking; Buon Fresco: calcium hydroxide slaked lime plaster Ca(OH)2 reacting with atmospheric CO2 to form calcium carbonate CaCO3 limestone rock matrix, trapping mineral pigments permanently inside the physical wall",
  }[reg=facet],
}

^"pigment_chemistry_and_historical_palette"{
  "prehistoric_ochres_to_precious_minerals": .{
    paleolithic_earth_ochres: "Iron oxides (hematite alpha-Fe2O3 red, goethite FeO(OH) yellow, manganese dioxide MnO2 black); ground on stone palettes and bound with animal fat or saliva in Chauvet and Lascaux caves (36,000 BCE)",
    lapis_lazuli_and_royal_ultramarine: "Natural ultramarine extracted from metamorphic lazurite in Badakhshan, Afghanistan; intense pure blue caused by trapped trisulfur radical anions (S3^-) within aluminosilicate sodalite zeolite cages; more costly than gold in Renaissance Europe, reserved by strict legal contract for the Virgin Mary's robes",
    toxic_historical_pigments: "Lead White (basic lead carbonate 2PbCO3*Pb(OH)2, unmatched handling and warm opacity, but chronic lead poisoning among artists); Vermilion (mercuric sulfide HgS); Orpiment (arsenic trisulfide As2S3)",
  }[reg=facet],
  "synthetic_pigment_revolutions": .{
    egyptian_blue_and_prussian_blue: "Egyptian Blue (calcium copper tetrasilicate CaCuSi4O10, synthesized 2600 BCE); Prussian Blue (ferric ferrocyanide Fe4[Fe(CN)6]3, accidentally synthesized by Diesbach in Berlin 1704 as the first modern laboratory pigment)",
    the_nineteenth_century_aniline_explosion: "William Henry Perkin (1856) synthesizing Mauveine from coal tar, launching the synthetic organic dye industry; Cadmium yellows (CdS) and synthetic French Ultramarine (Guimet 1828); Titanium Dioxide (1920s) replacing toxic lead white globally",
  }[reg=facet],
}

^"world_history_and_the_plein_air_liberation"{
  "renaissance_workshops_to_industrial_paint_tubes": .{
    the_renaissance_bottega_grinding: "Fifteenth-century apprentice painters spending years hand-grinding toxic minerals on porphyry slabs; storing mixed oil paint in fragile, dried pig bladders punctured with bone tacks that rapidly oxidized and spoiled",
    the_rand_collapsible_metal_tube_1841: "American portrait painter John Goffe Rand patenting the collapsible tin paint tube with screw cap; preserving wet oil paint indefinitely and enabling portable artistic practice; Renoir's declaration: 'Without paint in tubes, there would have been no Cezanne, no Monet, no Pissarro, and no Impressionism'",
    the_impressionist_plein_air_explosion: "Monet, Renoir, and Sisley leaving dark academic studios to set up easels outdoors under open skies; capturing transient atmospheric light, railway steam, and water ripples with vivid synthetic cadmiums and cobalts",
  }[reg=facet],
}

^"wonder_probes"{
  #>wonder_mat_paint_1
  ?["Why does the trisulfur radical anion in lapis lazuli generate the most intense blue in art history, yet decompose instantly when exposed to mild acidic vinegar?"]{
    #:depth #!molecular ~#lens(zeolite_cage_radical_stabilization)
    ~#hypothesis: "The S3^- radical anion is an intrinsically unstable free radical that would instantly react and quench in open liquid solution. In natural ultramarine, it is physically isolated and stabilized inside the rigid aluminosilicate sodalite cages of the lazurite zeolite crystal lattice, allowing electronic transitions between molecular orbitals to absorb red-yellow light (~600nm) and transmit pure blue. Acid penetrates the porous cage, protonating the radical into hydrogen sulfide gas (H2S) and bleaching the crystal white."
    !probe{ "Measure optical absorption spectra and sulfur K-edge X-ray absorption fine structure (XAFS) during acid-induced fading of Renaissance ultramarine pigment samples." }
    $%[crystallography.s3_radical_cage_retention_energy_ev, spectroscopy.ultramarine_blue_absorption_peak_nm]
  }
}
